A||||3||Avery Montembeault||tranny/clutch problems||||1||
Z||000000||Avery Montembeault||12-14-2001||04:11 PM||||I recently took my tranny off to replace the clutch. installation was no problem, however when i finally got everything back into place and was bleeding the slave cylinder, I ran into a problem, the slave does not move back into normal position. now, the rod that pushes the clutch fork moves back and forth just fine, so its my guess that its actually the fork that is not moving to its normal position. my only guesses for this is maybe the spring in the throwout bearing poped off and now doesnt reset the clutch fork. are there any other springs or systems that might be the problem though? I'd like to avoid droppng everything and taking it all apart again if possible, but if its what i got to do then its what I got to do...anybody else have a simerlar experience? oh yeah, its an izuzu 5 speed on a 4 banger, 1988 model coupe||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPAW||12-14-2001||04:33 PM||||When you say normal position - do you meen with the slave cylinder completely retracted?<P>The slave cylinder should push out until it engages with the clutch fork which pushes it against the clutch fingers releasing the clutch.<BR>When the clutch is released the fingers on the pressure plate are the only thing that pushes the slave back in -there is no other spring.<BR>With a new clutch the fingers will lie much flatter on the pressure plate and the release fork will not move back as much.  It still uses the same amount of travel just farther out on the slave cylinder.<BR>As the clutch wears the fingers will start to point away from the flywheel and gradually push the slave cylinder farther and farther in - this is what makes the clutch self adjusting.<P>As long as the rod from the slave cylinder is remaining in contact with the release fork when the clutch pedal is released and the clutch is disengaging normally everything should be o.k.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000002||Avery Montembeault||12-14-2001||05:34 PM||||ok, but I dont have any pressure when I step on the clutch pedal...is this normal then for a new clutch? I had just assumed that lack of pressure was because the slave piston remained extended||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPAW||12-14-2001||07:24 PM||||You may still have some air in the lines.  With air in the lines the cylinder will still move until it contacts the fork but will be unable apply any pressure (I am assuming that when you push the pedal the fork is not moving at all). It takes ALOT of pressure for the for the fork to move.<BR>I bled my system by forcing the slave cylinder to the fully retracted position and using a cheep vacuum bleeder from an auto parts store.  Because the line is so long and the weather was cool it took about 20 minutes to finally suck all the air out and then it worked perfectly. (I got in a hurry and sucked the master dry doubling the total time)<BR>Judging from what I have read in this forum - bleeding the Fiero clutch hydraulics can be a real pain.<BR>You can find alot of discussions about bleeding the hydraulics in the archives and V8 Archie has some good instructions on his website.<P>If the clutch was releasing and engaging correctly (other than being worn) before you changed it I would say air is the problem.<P>Paul||||reg||1
